    <contenttext>
        <h2 style="margin-top:10px;">Experiment 1 : Buck Converter</h2>
        <br>
        <strong>Concept</strong>
        <p>
            DC-DC Converters are power electronics circuits that convert a dc input voltage to a different dc output voltage level.
        </p>
        <center>
            <img alt="banner-buckConverter" class="img-responsive" id="headerMainImage" src="images/experiments/buckConverter/banner.jpg">
            <p>Figure 1: A buck converter on a printed circuit board</p>
        </center>

        <!-- Start drop-down Advantages DC-DC -->
        <p>
            <a class="collapsed" data-toggle="collapse" href="#advantages-dcdc">
                <span class="glyphicon glyphicon-chevron-right"></span>
                Advantages of DC-DC Converter
            </a>
        </p>
        <div class="collapse" id="advantages-dcdc" style="height: 0px;">
            <ul>
                <li>High efficiency.</li>
                <li>Smaller components and less thermal management.</li>
                <li>Output voltage can be either higher or lower than the input voltage.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <!-- End drop-down Advantages DC-DC -->

        <!-- Start drop-down Theory -->
        <p>
            <a class="collapsed" data-toggle="collapse" href="#theory">
                <span class="glyphicon glyphicon-chevron-right"></span>
                Theory
            </a>
        </p>
        <div class="collapse" id="theory" style="height: 0px;">
            <p align="justify">
                A buck converter’s output voltage is always lower than the input voltage. Figure 2 shows the schematics of a buck converter.
                It has a dc input voltage, a transistor working as a switch, an inductor and a capacitor forming a low pass
                filter to smooth out the output voltage, and a load resistor. The diode provides a path for the inductor
                current when the switch is opened and is reverse biased when the switch is closed.
            </p>
            <center>
                <img alt="schematics-buckConverter" class="img-responsive" src="images/experiments/buckConverter/fig2.jpg">
                <p>Figure 2. Schematics of a buck converter</p>
            </center>
            <p>When the switch is closed, the diode is reverse biased. The equivalent circuit is shown in Figure 3.</p>
            <center>
                <img alt="equivalentClosed-buckConverter" class="img-responsive" src="images/experiments/buckConverter/fig3.jpg">
                <p>Figure 3. Equivalent circuit when the switch is closed</p>
            </center>
            <p>When the switch is open, the diode is forward biased. The equivalent circuit is shown in Figure 4.</p>
            <center>
                <img alt="equivalentOpen-buckConverter" class="img-responsive" src="images/experiments/buckConverter/fig4.jpg">
                <p>Figure 4. Equivalent circuit when the switch is open</p>
            </center>
            <p>Output voltage of a buck converter is controlled by a pulse width modulated (PWM) signal. By adjusting the
                duty cycle, we can obtain desired output voltage.</p>
            <center>
                <img alt="fig5-buckConverter" class="img-responsive" src="images/experiments/buckConverter/fig5.jpg">
            </center>
        </div>
        <!-- End drop-down Theory -->

        <!-- Start drop-down Learning Objective -->
        <p>
            <a class="collapsed" data-toggle="collapse" href="#learning-objectives">
                <span class="glyphicon glyphicon-chevron-right"></span>
                Learning Objective
            </a>
        </p>
        <div class="collapse" id="learning-objectives" style="height: 0px;">
            <ol>
                <li>Characteristics of buck converter</li>
                <li>Capacitor and inductor selection</li>
                <li>Output voltage and current</li>
                <li>Continuous and discontinuous mode</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <!-- End drop-down Learning Objective -->

        <!-- Start drop-down Materials and Equipment -->
        <p>
            <a class="collapsed" data-toggle="collapse" href="#materials-equipment">
                <span class="glyphicon glyphicon-chevron-right"></span>
                Materials and Equipment
            </a>
        </p>
        <div class="collapse" id="materials-equipment" style="height: 0px;">
            <ul>
                <li>Power Supply</li>
                <li>Capacitor</li>
                <li>Inductor</li>
                <li>Transistor</li>
                <li>Diode</li>
                <li>Resistor</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <!-- End drop-down Materials and Equipment -->

        <!-- Start drop-down Procedure -->
        <p>
            <a class="collapsed" data-toggle="collapse" href="#procedure">
                <span class="glyphicon glyphicon-chevron-right"></span>
                Procedure
            </a>
        </p>
        <div class="collapse" id="procedure" style="height: 0px;">
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Make connections of power supply, capacitor, inductor, transistor and load resistor to build the buck converter.
                    <br>Vin = 50 V, L = 400 μH, C = 100 μF, f = 20 kHz, R = 20 Ω
                </li>
                <li>Check the circuit connections according to the schematics.</li>
                <li>Varying duty cycle</li>
                <li>Set the duty cycle at 40%, and switching frequency at 20 kHz.</li>
                <li>Observe and measure the output voltage.</li>
                <li>Vary the duty cycle from 20% to 80% with the interval of 10%.</li>
                <li>Measure the output voltage for each of the duty cycle. Compare the measurement with theoretical calculation.</li>
                <li>Varying switching frequency
                    <ol type="I">
                        <li>Set the duty cycle at 40%, and switching frequency at 20 kHz.</li>
                        <li>Observe and measure the output voltage ripple. Compare the measurement with theoretical calculation.</li>
                        <li>Increase the switching frequency to 50 kHz. Observe and measure the output voltage ripple. Compare
                            the measurement with theoretical calculation.</li>
                        <li>Decrease the switching frequency to 10 kHz. Observe and measure the output voltage ripple. Compare
                            the measurement with theoretical calculation.</li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
                <li>Varying load resistor
                    <ol type="I">
                        <li>Set the duty cycle at 40%, and switching frequency at 20 kHz.</li>
                        <li>Measure the load current and output voltage when the load resistance is 20 Ω.</li>
                        <li>Measure the load current and output voltage when the load resistance is 40 Ω.</li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <!-- End drop-down Procedure -->
    </contenttext>

